{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on candidate 99556064, the design-only host-boundary proposal host-boundary-proposal.md at SHA-256 7593d61b66da6d058bf1252261d2a6ddea94c8521bd4b0340ec9800a3862119c, under design child #4281 (request d0c377e9) and my review promise on request 27355e4e. All 14 recorded source identities verify byte-exact: the eight repository files equal main b583cdc4c3bcb1f6359f3298c067ae2298b02ca7 and the six go1.26.7 toolchain files equal their recorded hashes.\n\nArchitecture. The affected layers are the definition plane and reusable compiler, core evaluation and codecs, the data-plane transaction owner, and engine scheduling and activation. The proposal's map of each is accurate at the manifest commit. It preserves the core/host separation of notes/2026-08-28-tailapp-architecture.md@b583cdc:96-108 and places the coordinator outside the engine and projection critical sections rather than inside them, which is the correct disposition given that internal/engine/engine.go holds e.mu from :359 through the Process call at :413 and internal/projection/projection.go holds p.mu from :403 across the whole event transaction. The stage split is preserved and the ten-component obligation of #4043 is correctly reconciled with the nine components presently at main (jsonataddl/identity.go:21-31; internal/profile/runtime.go:24-37). One contract change is not carried through: making the admitted evaluator a process-wide singleton with serialized epochs (lines 12 and 22) narrows the public reusable module's contract so that no consumer may hold two independent allowance domains in one process. That consequence appears nowhere in the document and nowhere in the adoption paragraph, and it must, because the architecture note positions jsonataddl as a reusable module for external consumers. The adoption wording at line 73 also incorporates every load-bearing obligation by reference without naming the document hash; it must carry the SHA-256 so a ratifier adopts a pinned object rather than a prose description.\n\nSecurity. The trust and authority boundaries are handled well. The opaque generation-bound lease refuses copied-handle reuse and nested acquisition, a caller-supplied claim that collection finished is explicitly insufficient, collection is deliberately not cancellable so that cancellation cannot reopen admission, and the document states plainly that this is source-reviewed trusted integration and not protection against arbitrary Go code in the same process. No wire authority, provider privilege or SQLite extension is added, and no GC-derived value enters a semantic decision. Two gaps remain. The availability argument never states what bounds an adversary's control of epoch duration: it is the per-event W/A/D meter, and that is the reason process-wide serialization is not an open denial-of-service surface. And the containment rule that results leave an epoch only into a reserved bounded slot is enforced by an acceptance test rather than by the API: jsonataddl/evaluate.go:41-46 hands the host a plain struct of unbounded maps and slices. Neither is an unresolved defect in the proposal's own terms, but both belong in the text. No security defect requiring refusal.\n\nSimplification. One right-hand term of the induction can be deleted rather than proved. Qpool exists because one collection leaves a sync.Pool victim generation, correctly observed at line 43. Two consecutive collections with no intervening pool use leave every pool with a nil primary and a nil victim (sync/pool.go:265-280), which removes Qpool and its bounded-population obligation at the cost of one extra collection; the acceptance check must then pin quiescence, since a pool touched between the two collections repopulates through Get. Separately, lines 12 and 22 restate the same ownership constraint from two angles and line 18 repeats the candidate's own disclaimer; both can be trimmed without weakening any condition. Nothing else is fluff.\n\nWhat I checked. I read the proposal line by line against the source at the manifest commit and confirmed every cited entry point. The occupancy induction is structurally non-circular: charging each nonempty small-object span to one contained object is an injection into the retained-object population, so the span count is bounded by L rather than by pages, and line 55 explicitly forbids substituting the span lemma back into Qmax. I found no claim that the numerical terms or limiter reachability are proved; lines 5, 16, 41, 49, 55 and 63 each hold that line, and line 16 preserves #4281's missing-implication-versus-counterexample distinction exactly. The single slip is the induction's base case: line 49 says \"after a completed sweep\" and line 41 says runtime.GC \"completes or waits for its sweep\", but both sweep-completion loops in runtime/mgc.go:564 and :579 exit early when another cycle has begun, and the base case survives only because gcStart runs finishsweep_m under stop-the-world at runtime/mgc.go:868-871 and only while the other-actor allocation bound of line 13 holds. State that limit and its discharge. Three evidence corrections are also required: the page unit of 8192 and the largest small-class span both come from internal/runtime/gc/sizeclasses.go (PageShift = 13 at :91, MaxSizeClassNPages = 10 at :93), which is not in the manifest and is only forwarded by the manifest's runtime/malloc.go at :117-118; and the c9c774d2 helper carries a behavioural claim at line 32 without a recorded identity. I verified that claim independently against jsonataddl/source_snapshot.go at the tip of request/i7-metered-core and it is true, but the identity must be recorded. Smaller citation corrections: engine.go:362-413 begins at a closing brace and excludes the e.mu.Lock at :359 that the claim asserts; load.go:93 precedes the walk at :94, and the sharper finding there is that fs.ReadFile at :111 materializes a whole element before the bound checks at :115 and :122 and the ownership copy at :125, so the core itself violates charge-before-allocation at a site the host boundary does not fix; the all-four-targets claim rests on one hashed toolchain module; and line 26's correct preemptible-frame rule should cite runtime/mgc.go:738-743 and the two-minute worst case at runtime/proc.go:6476 and appear in the acceptance list. Stage 2 should also name the forced projection upgrade-or-reset that changing host.orchestration implies (internal/profile/runtime.go:18-23, current value two-stage-txn/3 at :34), and internal/projection/projection.go:428 records an unbounded wrapped error string, the concrete site the fixed-bounded-diagnostics requirement at line 34 is aimed at. I ran nothing: no build, no test, no collector experiment. I could not verify the bebeed31 evaluator revision (absent from every local repository), byte-identity of the runtime source across the other three toolchain modules, or the adopted four-target profile, and I say so rather than infer it.\n\nThe design itself is sound, honestly scoped and worth adopting once corrected. Every finding is a small, mechanical correction to the text, the manifest or the adoption wording; none requires reworking the boundary, the state machine or the induction. Republish at a new hash and request fresh review. Builder ratifies this verdict and reports the design child to Hugh; adoption remains distinct.\n\nFiling note: this verdict is filed as a report on my review promise with body.outcome=changes-requested rather than body.verdict, because the kernel routes a body.verdict report to gs review, and gs review binds only to an artifact-kind primary (it answered 'artifact event is unknown' for the candidate assert 99556064, which owes no Git artifact by the request's own terms). The text is the verdict; the candidate and the exact attachment hash are named above and in the body.","body":{"attachment_sha256":"7593d61b66da6d058bf1252261d2a6ddea94c8521bd4b0340ec9800a3862119c","candidate":"git:sha1:da732b0bdaa